  "query_preview": "..\nFriday night through Monday: An upper-level ridge shifts into the \nPacific Northwest as an upper-level low travels eastward across \nsouthern Idaho into Montana and Wyoming. This will promote mostly \nclear skies, light winds, and above normal temperatures. \nTemperatures will trend warmer and drier through the weekend with \nwidespread highs in the upper 70s to low 80s Sunday and Monday. The \nupper-level ridge begins to weaken on Monday as a frontal system \napproaches the British Columbia coast.\n\nTuesday through Friday: Ensembles are in solid agreement for a \nfrontal system offshore, though they diverge on if it will move\nonshore and begin the transition into a more active period for \nthe Inland Northwest. By Thursday, most ensembles (87%) keep the\nridge over the Inland Northwest, with a small subset of members\n(13%) showing a shortwave moving through. By Friday, 62% of \nensembles show an upper-level trough moving into the region with\nthe other 38% keep the upper-level ridge in place. This has \nresulted in a large amount of uncertainty in the forecast later \nin the week with a 14 to 17 degree spread in the 25th to 75th \npercentile high temperatures across the Inland Northwest. If the\nupper-level trough moves into the region, highs may be in the \nlow to mid 60s with precipitation, but if the upper-level ridge \nremains in place, drier weather with highs in the mid 70s to low\n80s could continue. /vmt",
